00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.170
Hi, I'm Erik Voss and Wonder Woman was
one of the most enjoyable superhero

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:08.280
movies in recent years. I feel like a big
reason for that is the music. Like, a lot

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.639
of superhero movie themes these days
tend to blend together, but Wonder

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:15.990
Woman's badass cameo and new theme song in Batman vs Superman last year

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:22.430
definitely saved the day and some would
say maybe saved the whole movie.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.210
And in this video, I'm going to break
down why exactly this music is so

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:35.399
effective, and how exactly the team
behind wonder woman used it to elevate the

00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:39.540
film as a whole. So, it all started back
in the early days of developing Batman

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.190
vs Superman. Hans Zimmer and Junkie XL returned to compose the score for the

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.850
movie. They also composed Man of Steel
and the Christopher Nolan Dark Knight

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.570
trilogy. Now, these two guys are
responsible for composing new distinct

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.649
themes for Batman and Wonder Woman, and Zimmer said that since pretty much every

00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:59.159
superhero movie that came before had
been so masculine and male-generated, he

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:02.489
wanted Wonder Woman's melody to be more uniquely feminine. Specifically, he said

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:06.659
that he was looking for something that
sounded like a banshee wail. And after a

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:10.229
hundred thousand experiments that went
wrong he turned to his friend, Tina Guo,

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:14.280
who plays the electric cello. Zimmer
described Guo as a version of Wonder

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.689
Woman herself. In conversation she's
elegant, regal and polite, but when she

00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:22.619
picks up her cello, all hell breaks loose.
He compared her performance actually to

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:27.060
a warrior, holding the cello like a sword.
Tina Guo actually used this comparison

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.290
as inspiration for a music video that
she recently released for the Wonder

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.350
Woman theme, which was originally titled
issue with you in the Batman vs Superman

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:38.970
soundtrack. Check out how Guo moves with so much aggression and power. The colors

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.840
of blue and red evoke the colors of
Wonder Woman's armor. And notice how they

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.140
covered her skin in clay for these
close-ups. Guo said that this was an

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.250
intentional nod to Diana's creation
story - she was crafted from clay. So, Guo

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.510
was kind of an actress herself in this
song. She injects so much passion and

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.560
fury into the melody. So, when Wonder
Woman comes to the rescue mid-battle in

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.760
Batman vs Superman, the electric cello
follows her like a screaming Banshee

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.880
war cry.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.640
But, when you break down this riff
melodically, there may be an even deeper

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.870
reason it's such a rallying fight song.
Notice that this melody is composed of

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:24.290
four notes E G B B-flat, that creates
dissonance, then resolving a half step

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.730
higher back to a B. Now if this sounds
kind of familiar to you, that's because

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.810
it's kind of similar to the progression
used in Led Zeppelin's immigrant song.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:37.459
Robert Plant's opening wail uses this
same technique of dissonance to

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:43.000
resolution, bending from a C, a half step
higher, to a C-sharp.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.090
And yeah, over the years immigrant song
has become so iconic that we often

00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:00.370
associate Plant's electric wail as a
universal voice of unleashed fury.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.150
Most recently, the song was used in the
trailer for Thor Ragnarok, where it was

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.859
associated with the arrival of another
fierce female warrior with mythological

00:03:08.859 --> 00:03:16.840
origins, Hela. But, this song has also been
associated with a ton of badass ladies

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.680
over the years. In 2011, the trailer and
opening title sequence of David

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.859
Fincher's Girl with the Dragon Tattoo
featured a cover of immigrant song by

00:03:23.859 --> 00:03:26.969
Karen O from the Yeah Yeah Yeah's.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.840
But there's also a scene in the third Shrek
movie when Snow White sings immigrant

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:40.700
song while deploying woodland creatures
on her enemies. Now to be clear here, the

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.720
idea of dissonance and resolution is
another way of saying tension and

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.930
release, which yeah, is something that
appears in a lot of musical melodies.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:52.670
Here it's used to suspend us in the
state of disorder and unrest just long

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.120
enough until we reset on the other side.
